      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let $\\sqrt[3]{x} = a$ and $\\sqrt[3]{20 - x} = b$. Thus, $a + b = 2$ and $a^3 + b^3 = 20$.\n\nThe sum of cubes can be written:\n$a^3 + b^3 = (a + b)(a^2 - ab + b^2) = (a + b)((a + b)^2 - 3ab)$.\nIf $a$ and $b$ are both positive, this becomes $2((2)^2 - 3ab) = 8 -6ab = 20$. Thus $2ab = -6$, which gives $ab = -3$.\n\nWe are also given that $(a,b) + (20 - a, 20 - b) = (2, 20)$.\nBecause $a = 1 - b$, substitute into $a(20 - a) = -3$:\n$(20 - (1 - b))b = -3$\n$20b - b^2 = -3$\n$b(20 - b) = -3$\nThis gives two values of $b$, but we need two $b$'s such that $ab = -3$.\n$20 - b = -3$, so $b = 23$. Also, $b = 3$, but this would contradict the perfect roots condition, therefore cancelling the $23$ solution.\n\nIf we make $b = 3$, then $a = -1$.\n\nSubstituting $b = 3$ into $a + b = 2$, we get $2a = -1$, and so $a = -\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nBecause $a = -\\frac{1}{2}$, $x = -\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^3$. Notice $x = \\boxed{\\frac{1}{8} - 4}$.",
