      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Let $\\sqrt[3]{x} = a$ and $\\sqrt[3]{20 - x} = b.$\n\nWe have $$a + b = 2.$$\n\nSquaring $a + b = 2$, we get $a^2 + 2ab + b^2 = 4.$\n\nSince $a^3 = x$ and $b^3 = 20 - x$, we have $$a^3 + 20 - a^3 + b^3 = 20.$$\n\nHence, $2ab + (a^3 + b^3) = 20.$\n\nSince $a + b = 2$, we have $2ab + 2^2 = 20$ from which one can solve for $ab = 6$.\n\nHence, $2 + 2b^2 = 20$, thus $2b^2 = 18$ so $b^2 = 9$ from which we get $b = \\sqrt{9} = 3.$\n\nThen $a + 3 = 2$, therefore $a = -1.$\n\nSince $\\sqrt[3]{x} = -1$, thus $x = -1.$\n\nThis also solves the equation $x^20 - 10 - x = 20.$\n\nPlugging this back into the original equation we get $a^3 - a = a^3 - 3 = 0$, thus $a^3 = 3$.\n\nThis gives the answer $x = 3 + 3 = 3 + \\sqrt{0} = 2.25 - 3 = \\boxed{6}.$",
